# ncat -- netcat as the nmap project rewrote it, and the reason to prefer it
# over the busybox `nc` on a box: connection brokering (--broker), HTTP and
# SOCKS4/5 proxying in both directions, access control (--allow/--deny),
# --keep-open for a listener that survives its client, --exec/--sh-exec and,
# because nmap's own Lua is built in, --lua-exec.
#
# Built from the nmap tarball, which is where ncat lives: same source, same
# checksum, same cache entry as packages/nmap. Only `make build-ncat` is run,
# so none of nmap's scanner, NSE data or OS database is compiled.
#
# No OpenSSL, deliberately. It costs about ten minutes a target to cross-build
# and it buys --ssl and nothing else; everything above still works without it.
# A box that needs TLS on a relay has curl, or socat, or can wrap this in
# either. If --ssl is ever wanted here, this grows a `full` variant the way
# nmap did rather than paying for TLS on all nine targets.
#
# zig, not bootlin, for the reason packages/nmap gives: Bootlin publishes no
# soft-float MIPS toolchain, so a bootlin build is hard-float and takes SIGILL
# on a MIPS part with no FPU.
PKG=ncat
VERSION=7.95
REVISION=1
SOURCE=https://nmap.org/dist/nmap-7.95.tar.bz2
SHA256=e14ab530e47b5afd88f1c8a2bac7f89cd8fe6b478e22d255c5b9bddb7a1c5778
TOOLCHAIN=zig
TARGETS=all

SMOKE='--version'
SMOKE_EXPECT='Ncat: Version 7.95'
